{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>לֹא תִהְיֶה אַחֲרֵי רַבִּים וְגוֹ׳.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded this verse at length, but the verse does not accord with its plain sense. It appears proper to explain the verse according to what the Torah said: ״וְשָׁפְטוּ הָעֵדָה וְהִצִּילוּ״ וגו׳ (Numbers 35:24–25), and they said of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 2a) — a congregation that convicts and a congregation that acquits; but if they all began to convict, he is exempt (Sanhedrin 17a)." "And the verse comes here to remove two stumbling blocks by which a sage may be tripped up in the application of this law. The first: when such a case arrives and one of the judges sees that all his colleagues have opened to convict, and he thinks in his mind that he is innocent, he may reason cleverly, saying: ״If I say 'innocent,' behold, he will emerge guilty, for there is a convicting congregation and an acquitting congregation, and so my words will produce the opposite of my view. Therefore I will be clever and say 'guilty,' for thereby my reasoning will be fulfilled — that he emerge innocent by virtue of the rule that if all opened [to convict, he is acquitted], etc., and I will also fulfill the ethical discipline of nullifying my view before the view of the majority who say 'guilty,' and I will say 'guilty' with them.״ For this reason the verse came and commanded precisely concerning this not to do it, and it said <b>לֹא תִהְיֶה אַחֲרֵי רַבִּים לְרָעֹת</b> — meaning, when you hold one reasoning such that by stating your view this defendant will emerge guilty in his judgment, and in a case where they all said 'guilty' and you think 'innocent' — do not be clever to follow the majority who say 'guilty,' which is what it says <b>לְרָעֹת</b>, and you say like their view 'guilty,' contrary to what appears to you, in order that he emerge innocent according to your view. Rather, state your view according to what you know in the laws of the Torah, and you have no business being clever about the judgment, for HaShem — He is the Elohim, and this is what He said: if all opened to convict, he is innocent. HaShem did not say that this one will be acquitted unless to you he appears guilty and does not appear to you innocent — for then he is guilty according to the judgment of Elohim." "<b>Further,</b> there is a second stumbling block in this law in another manner: if it appears in the eyes of a sage that he is guilty, like all the judges who said 'guilty,' and he sees that if he says 'guilty,' behold, he will emerge innocent — contrary to his own words that he says 'guilty.' Therefore he may be clever and say 'innocent,' so that the judgment will incline after the majority who say 'guilty' and he will emerge guilty according to his reasoning. And this too involves a manipulation of the outcome at the conclusion of the judgment in order to incline after the majority. Therefore it said <b>וְלֹא תַעֲנֶה עַל רִיב</b> וגו׳ — meaning, do not give an answer regarding a case to incline <b>אַחֲרֵי רַבִּים</b> — meaning, that your intention in your answer is in order that it be as the judgment of a single and a majority; and this tells us that if he reveals his view as it actually is, there is no inclining after the majority here, but rather all are of one mind. And the verse explained the reason for its strictness about this matter and said <b>לְהַטֹּת</b> — meaning, when your intention is to incline from what is in your mind through examination of the Torah's judgment, that he is guilty, and you incline to say 'innocent' in order that he emerge guilty by inclining after the majority and emerge guilty — no; rather, state your view even though he will emerge innocent contrary to your view — HaShem acquitted him. And the reason for this law is that HaShem sits in the assembly of judges and He judges through their mouths; and when He sees a judgment on high and there is no means to acquit the innocent one, He contrives to have them all open to convict and commands that he emerge innocent thereby."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:32:47.851882223Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}